#{[{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097608} :activity/id #uuid "ccadebeb-ab4f-5e8c-b59d-112db894f229" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049885 :topic/id #uuid "1b27fe0a-58ff-57f2-8e77-9341dd7fc153" :topic/math-academy-id 1082} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097608 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097796 :tutorial/content "The region enclosed by the curves $y=x^4$ and $y=x$ is shown below. Suppose that this region is rotated $2\\pi$ radians about the horizontal line $y=-1$, generating a solid of revolution. How can we write an integral expression that gives the volume of the solid?\n\n![](images/18/1880138e4a20cb5a47ba89cb3177d5dc6e74f998d7fdd428cdded7af38cadd02.png)\n\nFirst, we want to determine the limits of integration. We find the intersections of the curves by solving the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx^{4} &= x \\\\\nx^{4} - x &= 0 \\\\\nx(x^{3} - 1) &= 0 \\\\\nx &= 0, 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, our limits of integration are from $x=0$ to $x=1$.\n\nNow, let's draw our vertical arrow through the given region. The arrow should be pointing away from the axis of rotation.\n\n![](images/49/49da0885bbf23e586b488e2b2a9bcaa0d923d3591833c9b8beb31a611c1d8b43.png)\n\nThe vertical arrow\n\n- *enters* the region through the inner curve $y=x^4$, and\n- *leaves* though the outer curve $y=x$.\n\nNow, we can express the volume of our solid as the difference between the volumes of the solids generated by the outer and inner curves, as follows:\n\n- **Step I.** Figure out the volume generated by the outer curve $y=x$.![](images/35/352ff26bb5dce0a923c102c4528bd89555b49203c212fb73a42cf31cdf8b226e.png)\n\nThe corresponding volume is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nV_{outer} &= π\\int_{0}^{1}[(upper) - (lower)]^{2}dx \\\\\n&= π\\int_{0}^{1}[(x) - (-1)]^{2}dx \\\\\n&= π\\int_{0}^{1}[x + 1]^{2}dx\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- **Step II.** Figure out the volume generated by the inner curve $y=x^4$.![](images/ee/ee0fa31b61acd4a1f2a9b6bb21dcd0e5eed3c529780fcb67096d40dccf90c20c.png)\n\nThe corresponding volume is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nV_{inner} &= π\\int_{0}^{1}[(upper) - (lower)]^{2}dx \\\\\n&= π\\int_{0}^{1}[(x^{4}) - (-1)]^{2}dx \\\\\n&= π\\int_{0}^{1}[x^{4} + 1]^{2}dx\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- **Step III.** Find the difference.\nFinally, we can write the volume of the original solid as\n$V|= V_{outer} - V_{inner}; = π\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{1}[x + 1]^{2}dx - π\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{1}[x^{4} + 1]^{2}dx; = π\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{1}([x + 1]^{2} - [x^{4} + 1]^{2})dx$." :tutorial/id #uuid "8e33de60-9df4-540e-b19b-f12088b349e2" :tutorial/math-academy-id 1064 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "626bee34-cf24-50f9-b6b3-1fcbce8fe1a3" :step/math-academy-id 2092 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097659}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097609 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096729 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096531 :question/id #uuid "b6dd2970-3cdc-58fc-8f28-0bfdcd738d6f" :question/math-academy-id "e-593"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e2e0268f-36a5-5f55-a0ae-9e0f34981dab" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097228} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097229}] :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Volume of a Solid Generated by Rotation About a Horizontal Line"} :step/id #uuid "52be5c09-e883-51da-a981-64e4b7986356" :step/math-academy-id 2093 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097610}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097610 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097797 :tutorial/content "Now, suppose that the region enclosed by the curves\n\n$$\ny=\\dfrac x2\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\ny=\\sqrt x\n$$\n\nis rotated $360^\\circ$ about the vertical line $x=-2$. Let's find the volume of the resulting solid.\n\n![](images/78/7876211e2781a079ac2444436e671e2d61c28ec09774c0acad65e96fb537927a.png)\n\nFirst, since we are rotating about a vertical line, we need to solve the equations of our curves for $x$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}y = \\frac{x}{2} & ⟹x = 2y \\\\ y = \\sqrt{x} & ⟹x = y^{2}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nNow, we want to determine the limits of integration. We find the intersections of the curves by solving the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2y &= y^{2} \\\\\ny^{2} - 2y &= 0 \\\\\ny(y - 2) &= 0 \\\\\ny &= 0, 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, our limits of integration are from $y=0$ to $y={2}$.\n\nNow, let's draw a horizontal arrow through the given region. The arrow should be pointing away from the axis of rotation.\n\n![](images/c3/c3fd84335240208fb158da9b67aaffa07cf490c67f8a77bf28947df998180b76.png)\n\nThe horizontal arrow\n\n- *enters* the region through the inner curve $x={y^2}$, and\n- *leaves* though the outer curve $x=2y$.\n\nNext, we can find the volume of our solid as the difference between the volumes of the solids generated by the outer and inner curves.\n\n- **Step I.** Figure out the volume generated by the outer curve $x=2y$.![](images/6a/6a075f9b5fb64923de7037b31ec6dd0408e88aa6d965ebb8b204b6c560e23ec1.png)\n\nThe corresponding volume is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nV_{outer} &= π\\int_{0}^{2}[(right) - (left)]^{2}dy \\\\\n&= π\\int_{0}^{2}[(2y) - (-2)]^{2}dy \\\\\n&= π\\int_{0}^{2}[2y + 2]^{2}dy\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- **Step II.** Figure out the volume generated by the inner curve $x=y^2$.![](images/cc/cca4c498956a16cb374a8d0b3341a09e52844026593557710525d07be79a233a.png)\n\nThe corresponding volume is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nV_{inner} &= π\\int_{0}^{2}[(right) - (left)]^{2}dy \\\\\n&= π\\int_{0}^{2}[(y^{2}) - (-2)]^{2}dy \\\\\n&= π\\int_{0}^{2}[y^{2} + 2]^{2}dy\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- **Step III.** Find the difference.Finally, we can write the volume of the original solid as\n$V|= V_{outer} - V_{inner}; = π\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{2}[2y + 2]^{2}dy - π\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{2}[y^{2} + 2]^{2}dy; = π\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{2}([2y + 2]^{2} - [y^{2} + 2]^{2})dy; = π\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{2}(-y^{4} + 8y)dy; = \\left.π(-\\frac{y^{5}}{5} + 4y^{2})\\right|_{0}^{2}; = π[(-\\frac{32}{5} + 16) - 0]; = \\frac{48π}{5}$." :tutorial/id #uuid "78a4b0ee-944d-5de3-846a-bf591d05387b" :tutorial/math-academy-id 1066 :tutorial/title "Rotation About a Vertical Line"} :step/id #uuid "ca0b12a1-0e89-5062-b87e-358443f928e2" :step/math-academy-id 2097 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097660}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097611 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096730 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096532 :question/id #uuid "b79b0d88-0b42-5223-927b-5a4023f6c329" :question/math-academy-id "e-594"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3c6aa618-4d2f-5ce2-9bb2-c6b528ec9b7b" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097208} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097209}] :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Volume of a Solid Generated by Rotation About a Vertical Line"} :step/id #uuid "bb053558-c7a0-533b-bcf7-e8427cfa707a" :step/math-academy-id 2098} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097659 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096764 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096569 :question/id #uuid "8e436e4c-45da-528c-885b-9a094919a652" :question/math-academy-id "e-7508"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e9016e61-3365-5f53-ae10-cc94e9c638f3" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048539} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048643}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097206} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097207}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying an Integral Representing the Volume of a Solid Generated by Rotation About a Horizontal Line"} :step/id #uuid "38143ed9-ca20-5c45-b750-274568e87032" :step/math-academy-id 27864 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097609}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097660 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096765 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096570 :question/id #uuid "d064d066-3bbe-5381-ab56-3fc407da144f" :question/math-academy-id "e-7509"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "26659894-bc6d-50fe-a5f8-029c962452cc" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097212} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097307} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186527958}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying an Integral Representing the Volume of a Solid Generated by Rotation About a Vertical Line"} :step/id #uuid "3a272f90-abf1-569a-853d-f9c7cfbd0534" :step/math-academy-id 27865 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097611}}] :activity/title "Volumes of Revolution Using the Washer Method: Rotation About Other Axes" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186096871}]}
